From abec9d086620045b746831ebd71fa6ad4869931c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 12:14:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Serve TileLang FP8 decode rows on race-free 16, 32 and 64-row
 tiles

Decode rows of the block-32 FP8 projections ran on one 64-row tile, so
one stream's six verified rows paid for 64 rows of tensor-core work and
lost to B12X's dense GEMM on the latency-bound projections. Decode rows
now run on the shortest of 16-, 32- and 64-row tiles that holds them,
each with its own swept configuration per TP4 shape (DECODE_FP8_CONFIGS),
including K blocks of 256.

The decode tiles run TileLang's unspecialized pipeline. With warp
specialization, the cp.async scale staging of short tiles raced on SM121:
identical inputs gave different, wrong results in up to every run of a
16-row tile, while the unspecialized pipeline never did and is as fast
for decode. Prefill keeps the warp-specialized pipeline, which is up to
17% faster there. Every tile accumulates K in the same order, so rows
keep the prefill kernel's bits on every route.

A prefill weight larger than L2 (the DSpark main projection, 39 MB at
TP4) was re-read from DRAM by every row of tiles; it now rasterizes in
panels of eight N tiles, which made a 33 MB projection's 4096-row GEMM
three times faster.

The decode tiles come from a sweep at the TP4 serving shapes (attention,
shared expert and DSpark projections) with the weight warm, cold and
racing its own L2 prefetch, keeping each tile's best worst case against
B12X.

The tests cover every tile height and the swizzled prefill tile against
the full batch bit for bit, and repeat the narrowest tile that raced.
